audio:
  - id: bg_music
    kind: music
    src: "audio/global_history_theme.mp3"
    at: 0.0
    duration: 198.3
    gain_db: -18.0
    duck: true

  - id: nar_01
    kind: narration
    src: "audio/nar_01.wav"
    at: 0.0
    duration: 25.9
    text: >-
      Coffee begins as an understorey shrub in the forests of southwestern
      Ethiopia, where Coffea arabica still grows wild. The story everyone
      tells is of a goatherd named Kaldi noticing his animals would not
      settle after eating the berries. It was first written down more than a
      thousand years after it supposedly happened, which is usually a sign
      that a story is doing work other than reporting.

  - id: nar_02
    kind: narration
    src: "audio/nar_02.wav"
    at: 26.3
    duration: 19.2
    text: >-
      Cultivation, as opposed to gathering, begins across the Red Sea in
      Yemen. By the fifteenth century Sufi orders were drinking it to stay
      awake through night prayers, and the port of Mocha was shipping it far
      enough that the name attached itself to the drink and never came off.

  - id: nar_03
    kind: narration
    src: "audio/nar_03.wav"
    at: 45.9
    duration: 17.3
    text: >-
      The coffeehouse as an institution appears in Constantinople in the
      fifteen fifties. Within a few decades there were hundreds of them, and
      they were closed and reopened repeatedly by authorities who understood
      perfectly well that the danger was not the coffee but the
      conversation.

  - id: nar_04
    kind: narration
    src: "audio/nar_04.wav"
    at: 63.6
    duration: 16.5
    text: >-
      Venetian merchants brought it into Europe through the Levant trade in
      the early sixteen hundreds. It arrived as an expensive curiosity sold
      by apothecaries, and became an ordinary drink only once the price fell
      far enough for ordinary people to argue over.

  - id: nar_05
    kind: narration
    src: "audio/nar_05.wav"
    at: 80.5
    duration: 18.0
    text: >-
      It was not welcomed everywhere. Coffee arrived in Europe attached to
      the Ottoman world, and that association was enough for some clergy to
      want it condemned outright. The story goes that Pope Clement the
      Eighth was asked to rule against it, tasted it first, and declined.

  - id: nar_06
    kind: narration
    src: "audio/nar_06.wav"
    at: 98.9
    duration: 16.9
    text: >-
      When the Ottoman army withdrew from Vienna in sixteen eighty-three it
      left supplies behind, coffee among them. The popular version credits a
      single heroic messenger; the licence records credit a merchant named
      Johannes Diodato, who opened the city's first coffeehouse two years
      later.

  - id: nar_07
    kind: narration
    src: "audio/nar_07.wav"
    at: 116.2
    duration: 16.1
    text: >-
      London's coffeehouses were nicknamed penny universities, because a
      penny bought entry and the argument was free. They specialised:
      shipping men gathered at one owned by Edward Lloyd, and the insurance
      market that grew out of that room still carries his name.

  - id: nar_08
    kind: narration
    src: "audio/nar_08.wav"
    at: 132.7
    duration: 26.7
    text: >-
      Coffee reached the Americas as a cutting. A French naval officer,
      Gabriel de Clieu, is said to have carried a seedling to Martinique in
      the seventeen twenties and shared his water ration with it when the
      voyage ran long. Whatever the truth of the ration, the plantations
      that followed were worked by enslaved people, and that is the part of
      the story the anecdote tends to crowd out.

  - id: nar_09
    kind: narration
    src: "audio/nar_09.wav"
    at: 159.8
    duration: 17.3
    text: >-
      From Martinique it spread across the Caribbean and into South America,
      and Brazil eventually grew more of it than everywhere else combined.
      That single-country dominance is what turned coffee from a luxury into
      a commodity, with prices set far from anyone who grew it.

  - id: nar_10
    kind: narration
    src: "audio/nar_10.wav"
    at: 177.5
    duration: 20.8
    text: >-
      The trade now runs the other way as well. Buyers go back to the
      Ethiopian forest for varieties nobody has planted at scale, because
      almost all the world's arabica descends from a very small founding
      population, and the diversity that might survive a warming climate is
      still sitting where the whole thing started.

media:
  - id: watermark
    kind: image
    src: "media/geostudio_logo.png"
    placement: bottom_right
    origin: upload
    credit: "GeoStudio"

  - id: plant_inset
    kind: image
    src: "media/gabriel_de_clieu.jpg"
    at: 133.7
    until: 158.9
    placement: full
    origin: upload
    caption: "Gabriel de Clieu sharing his water ration with the coffee seedling."

layers: []

scenes:
  - id: scene_01
    sequence: 1
    title: "Origins"
    place_id: pl_ethiopia
    basemap: carto-dark
    transition: cut
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 25.9, measured: 25.9, source: nar_01 }
    audio_ids: [nar_01]
    content:
      caption: "Coffea arabica grows wild in the Ethiopian highlands."
      narration: >-
        Coffee begins as an understorey shrub in the forests of southwestern
        Ethiopia, where Coffea arabica still grows wild. The story everyone
        tells is of a goatherd named Kaldi noticing his animals would not
        settle after eating the berries. It was first written down more than
        a thousand years after it supposedly happened, which is usually a
        sign that a story is doing work other than reporting.

  - id: scene_02
    sequence: 2
    title: "Across the Red Sea"
    place_id: pl_mocha
    basemap: carto-dark
    transition: fly
    delay: 0.4
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 19.2, measured: 19.2, source: nar_02 }
    audio_ids: [nar_02]
    content:
      caption: "Yemen, where coffee was first farmed and first traded."
      narration: >-
        Cultivation, as opposed to gathering, begins across the Red Sea in
        Yemen. By the fifteenth century Sufi orders were drinking it to stay
        awake through night prayers, and the port of Mocha was shipping it
        far enough that the name attached itself to the drink and never came
        off.

  - id: scene_03
    sequence: 3
    title: "The First Coffeehouses"
    place_id: pl_istanbul
    basemap: carto-dark
    transition: fly
    delay: 0.4
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 17.3, measured: 17.3, source: nar_03 }
    audio_ids: [nar_03]
    content:
      caption: "Constantinople, 1550s — coffee becomes a place, not just a drink."
      narration: >-
        The coffeehouse as an institution appears in Constantinople in the
        fifteen fifties. Within a few decades there were hundreds of them,
        and they were closed and reopened repeatedly by authorities who
        understood perfectly well that the danger was not the coffee but the
        conversation.

  - id: scene_04
    sequence: 4
    title: "Into Europe"
    place_id: pl_venice
    basemap: carto-dark
    transition: dissolve
    delay: 0.4
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 16.5, measured: 16.5, source: nar_04 }
    audio_ids: [nar_04]
    content:
      caption: "Venice, and the first European market."
      narration: >-
        Venetian merchants brought it into Europe through the Levant trade
        in the early sixteen hundreds. It arrived as an expensive curiosity
        sold by apothecaries, and became an ordinary drink only once the
        price fell far enough for ordinary people to argue over.

  - id: scene_05
    sequence: 5
    title: "Suspicion"
    basemap: carto-dark
    transition: fade
    delay: 0.4
    timing: { mode: fixed, intended: 18.0, measured: 18.0, source: nar_05 }
    audio_ids: [nar_05]
    content:
      caption: "Not accepted everywhere, and not immediately."
      narration: >-
        It was not welcomed everywhere. Coffee arrived in Europe attached to
        the Ottoman world, and that association was enough for some clergy
        to want it condemned outright. The story goes that Pope Clement the
        Eighth was asked to rule against it, tasted it first, and declined.

  - id: scene_06
    sequence: 6
    title: "The Siege of Vienna"
    place_id: pl_vienna
    basemap: carto-dark
    transition: fly
    delay: 0.4
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 16.9, measured: 16.9, source: nar_06 }
    audio_ids: [nar_06]
    content:
      caption: "1683 — the siege lifts, and the sacks stay."
      narration: >-
        When the Ottoman army withdrew from Vienna in sixteen eighty-three
        it left supplies behind, coffee among them. The popular version
        credits a single heroic messenger; the licence records credit a
        merchant named Johannes Diodato, who opened the city's first
        coffeehouse two years later.

  - id: scene_07
    sequence: 7
    title: "Penny Universities"
    place_id: pl_london
    basemap: carto-dark
    transition: fly
    delay: 0.4
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 16.1, measured: 16.1, source: nar_07 }
    audio_ids: [nar_07]
    content:
      caption: "London — a penny at the door, and whatever was being discussed."
      narration: >-
        London's coffeehouses were nicknamed penny universities, because a
        penny bought entry and the argument was free. They specialised:
        shipping men gathered at one owned by Edward Lloyd, and the
        insurance market that grew out of that room still carries his name.

  - id: scene_08
    sequence: 8
    title: "The Stolen Seedling"
    place_id: pl_martinique
    basemap: arcgis-satellite
    transition: fly
    delay: 0.4
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 26.7, measured: 26.7, source: nar_08 }
    audio_ids: [nar_08]
    media_ids: [plant_inset]
    camera:
      mode: 2d
      center: [-61.02, 14.64]
      zoom: 6
      easing: ease-in-out
      track:
        - at: 133.7
          center: [-40.0, 20.0]
          zoom: 3.2
          locked: true
        - at: 146.0
          center: [-52.0, 17.0]
          zoom: 4.5
        - at: 158.4
          center: [-61.02, 14.64]
          zoom: 6.0
          locked: true
    content:
      caption: "Martinique, 1720s — one plant crosses the Atlantic."
      narration: >-
        Coffee reached the Americas as a cutting. A French naval officer,
        Gabriel de Clieu, is said to have carried a seedling to Martinique
        in the seventeen twenties and shared his water ration with it when
        the voyage ran long. Whatever the truth of the ration, the
        plantations that followed were worked by enslaved people, and that
        is the part of the story the anecdote tends to crowd out.

  - id: scene_09
    sequence: 9
    title: "The Brazilian Century"
    place_id: pl_brazil
    basemap: arcgis-satellite
    transition: fly
    delay: 0.4
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 17.3, measured: 17.3, source: nar_09 }
    audio_ids: [nar_09]
    content:
      caption: "By 1900, Brazil grew most of the world's coffee."
      narration: >-
        From Martinique it spread across the Caribbean and into South
        America, and Brazil eventually grew more of it than everywhere else
        combined. That single-country dominance is what turned coffee from a
        luxury into a commodity, with prices set far from anyone who grew
        it.

  - id: scene_10
    sequence: 10
    title: "Back to the Forest"
    place_id: pl_ethiopia
    basemap: carto-dark
    transition: dissolve
    delay: 0.4
    timing: { mode: derived, intended: 20.8, measured: 20.8, source: nar_10 }
    audio_ids: [nar_10]
    content:
      caption: "The genetic reservoir is still where it started."
      narration: >-
        The trade now runs the other way as well. Buyers go back to the
        Ethiopian forest for varieties nobody has planted at scale, because
        almost all the world's arabica descends from a very small founding
        population, and the diversity that might survive a warming climate
        is still sitting where the whole thing started.

